@page "/auto-pause"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>Automatic circuit pause</PageTitle>

<h1>Automatic circuit pause</h1>

<p>
    Preview 6 added <code>Circuit.RequestCircuitPauseAsync</code> so the <em>server</em> could
    pause a circuit on demand. Preview 7 adds the matching client-driven path: the circuit
    pauses itself after the browser tab has been hidden for a configurable delay, releasing the
    SignalR connection and its server-side memory until the user comes back
    (<a href="https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/67098">dotnet/aspnetcore #67098</a>,
    <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/67045">#67045</a>).
</p>

<p>
    The behavior ships in a separate opt-in package,
    <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.AutoPause</code>, and is configured on the
    render mode's browser options. See <code>Program.cs</code>:
</p>

<pre><code>app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()
    .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode()
    .WithBrowserOptions(options =&gt;
    {
        options.AddAutoPause(pause =&gt;
        {
            pause.Enabled = true;                          // default
            pause.HiddenDelay = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30);  // default is 2 minutes
        });
    });</code></pre>

<h2>Try it</h2>

<ol>
    <li>Open the browser dev tools console.</li>
    <li>Increment the counter so you can tell live state from restored state.</li>
    <li>Switch to another tab (or minimize) and wait 30 seconds.</li>
    <li>
        Come back. The console shows the pause deferral running, then the circuit pausing and
        resuming. The counter value survives, because pause preserves circuit state.
    </li>
</ol>

<p>Counter: <strong>@count</strong></p>
<button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="() => count++">Increment</button>

<h2>Deferring a pause</h2>

<p>
    Auto-pause defers itself while a pause would lose data or interrupt work in progress. The
    shipped checks are: a text input or <code>contenteditable</code> element whose value differs
    from its default, an <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;video&gt;</code> element that is
    playing unmuted, an open Picture-in-Picture window, a held
    <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/API/Web_Locks_API">Web Lock</a>, and any
    in-flight circuit activity (JS interop calls, stream transfers, queued renders).
</p>

<p>
    Type something into the box below, then hide the tab: the pause is deferred for as long as
    the field stays dirty. Clear it and the pause proceeds.
</p>

<input class="form-control mb-3" placeholder="Type here to block auto-pause" />

<p>
    Apps can add their own deferral with a <strong>circuit handler</strong> that implements
    <code>onCircuitPausing(signal)</code>. Blazor awaits every registered handler before it
    pauses &mdash; for both auto-pause and server-initiated pauses. Register it from a JS
    initializer (see <code>wwwroot/BlazorFeatures.lib.module.js</code>):
</p>

<pre><code>export function beforeWebStart(options) {
  options.circuit ??= {};
  options.circuit.circuitHandlers ??= [];
  options.circuit.circuitHandlers.push({
    onCircuitPausing: async (signal) =&gt; {
      // Persist in-progress state. `signal` aborts if the pause is cancelled,
      // for example because the tab became visible again.
      await savePendingWork(signal);
    },
  });
}</code></pre>

@code {
    private int count;
}
